On 2009-08-01, Matt Weiner <matt@mattweiner.net> wrote:
> About ?oSM vs. Elbereth, isn't the main advantage of
> the scroll the 0% failure rate?  If you need a monster to
> stop bothering you right away, writing in the dust might
> be risky, but the scroll is bound to work. 

It's important to note there's a big difference between writing in the
dust (roughly 70% success rate) and engraving with an athame/wand of
digging or burning with a wand of fire/lightning.

The engraving and burning methods both succeed 100% of the time as long as
the engraver is not otherwise impaired (engraving while hallucinating is
"low probability", for example).  

This would seem to indicate that the scroll is slightly better since it 
works even while hallucinating/blind, but the scroll is such a scarce 
resource and the deficiencies in question so rare that it's simply better 
to use Elbereth whenever you need the effect, and keep one blessed 
scroll in your bag for super-corner-case situations.

>?oSM doesn't work against Elbereth-resistant
> monsters. I kind of think it should, because it's a scarce
> resource and Elbereth can be written any time, but I'm
> not the DevTeam.)

In Spork, the scroll of scare monster cannot be resisted by monsters who
might otherwise resist the effects of Elbereth, including monsters who
by virtue of their high level are effectively immune to Elbereth -- such
as Demogorgon.  However, monsters which would never have respected
Elbereth (@, A, quest nemesis/leaders, minotaurs, etc.) in the first
place will still not respect the scroll.

